{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>YHWH Elohim</b> — compassionate in judgment; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 3:24). But the Rav did not take note that the first Name is written with אל\"ף דל\"\"ת (i.e., as אדנ\"י) and the second is written with יו\"ד ה\"א (i.e., as יהוה), concerning which the Sages said: every place where ה׳ is said is the attribute of mercy; אלהים is the attribute of judgment. But the names mentioned here — Adonai — is in the attribute of mercy. Thus they said in Tanḥuma: He said to Him, Master of the worlds, if You demand of me in justice, here I am; and if You do not demand of me in justice, have compassion upon me. Just as Abraham said, \"YHWH Elohim, what will You give me\" etc. (Genesis 15:2), and also in the case of Abraham they are written the first as אל\"ף דל\"\"ת and the second as יו\"ד ה\"א. And the meaning of the verse is understood accordingly: for he said <b>וָאֶתְחַנַּן</b> (\"and I implored\") and did not say <b>וָאֶתְפַּלֵּל</b> (\"and I prayed\"), because he was speaking in supplication. And he said Adonai, in whom is mercy — You have begun to show Your servant: this was the beginning of my prophecy, when You showed me Your glory, as it is written, \"And Moses hid his face, for he feared to look upon Elohim\" (Exodus 3:6) — along with the greatness and the might. And You showed me to know who is El in heaven and on earth, that from Him come Your great deeds and Your mighty acts. From here you will further understand with what the glorious Name became angered against him. But the language of the Sifre (Va'etḥanan 24) is: every place where ה׳ is said is the attribute of mercy, as it is said, \"YHWH, El, compassionate and gracious\" (Exodus 34:6); אלהים is the attribute of judgment, as it is said, \"until the matter comes before Elohim\" (Exodus 22:8). And the intention is to explain that the second Name here is the attribute of mercy, and it is the special Name, while all the other names are the attribute of judgment, such as אלהים, which is also a name for judges, and all the more so אל\"ף דל\"\"ת, which is an expression of masters. Now, he completed the reproofs with this, for he informed them that their fathers brought evil upon themselves, and upon him too they brought the punishment that they would not enter the Land; but they, the children, will enter and inherit it, if they are not like their fathers, a stubborn and rebellious generation. Therefore he now begins to warn them concerning the general principles of the commandments, that they shall not add to them and shall not subtract from them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The text refers to how the divine names are vocalized/read: the first Name ה' (YHWH) is pointed with the vowels of אדני, and the second אלהים is pointed with the vowels of אלהים read as אֱלֹהִים. Ramban uses the orthographic shorthand אל\"ף דל\"\"ת and יו\"ד ה\"א to distinguish the vowel pointing; these are left in Hebrew as they carry the technical argument about consonantal vs. vocalized forms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:57.095769515Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}